    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} - 4 x y + y=-8.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,-1).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} - 4 x y + y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-8.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,-1)=f_y(-1,-1)i-f_x(-1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} - 4 x y + y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} - 4 x y + y)\\,j \\\\ &=(1 - 4 x)\\,i-(- 6 x - 4 y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,-1)&=(1 - 4 \\left(-1\\right))\\,i-(- 6 \\left(-1\\right) - 4 \\left(-1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(5)\\,i-(10)\\,j \\\\ &=5\\, i-10\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $5\\, i-10\\, j.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
