    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y=-20.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-2,2).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-20.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-2,2)=f_y(-2,2)i-f_x(-2,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ &=(4 x + 4)\\,i-(- 6 x + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ t(-2,2)&=(4 \\left(-2\\right) + 4)\\,i-(- 6 \\left(-2\\right) + 4 \\left(2\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(-4)\\,i-(20)\\,j \\\\ &=-4\\, i-20\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-4\\, i-20\\, j.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
