    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} - 4 x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=4 + 4 e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,e).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, if we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} - 4 x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4 + 4 e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,e)=f_y(-1,e)i-f_x(-1,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^{2} - 4 x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^{2} - 4 x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(- 4 x - \\frac{3 x}{y})\\,i-(2 x - 4 y - 3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(-1,e)&=(- 4 \\left(-1\\right) - \\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(2 \\left(-1\\right) - 4 \\left(e\\right) - 3 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(\\frac{3}{e} + 4)\\,i-(- 4 e - 5)\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{3}{e} + 4\\right)\\, i+\\left(5 + 4 e\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(\\frac{3}{e} + 4\\right)\\, i+\\left(5 + 4 e\\right)\\, j.$",
