      ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $- 2 x^{2} + x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=-24 + 2 e^{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,e^{2}).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} + x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-24 + 2 e^{2}.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,e^{2})$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,e^{2})=f_y(2,e^{2})i-f_x(2,e^{2})j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,e^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 2 x^{2} + x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 2 x^{2} + x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(x - \\frac{4 x}{y})\\,i-(- 4 x + y - 4 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(2,e^{2})&=(\\left(2\\right) - \\frac{4 \\left(2\\right)}{\\left(e^{2}\\right)})\\,i-(- 4 \\left(2\\right) + \\left(e^{2}\\right) - 4 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e^{2}\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(2 - \\frac{8}{e^{2}})\\,i-(-16 + e^{2})\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(2 - \\frac{8}{e^{2}}\\right)\\, i+\\left(16 - e^{2}\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(2 - \\frac{8}{e^{2}}\\right)\\, i+\\left(16 - e^{2}\\right)\\, j.$",
